 gettext("<h3>Navigation and recording</h3><p>Settings lists Version, Connections and Import in that order, followed by Recovery and Android permissions. Time tracking starts enabled with fresh data; a saved off choice stays off. Its briefcase menu opens recording, settings and history. Complete a slider to start, pause, resume or finish; partial drags do nothing. History supports corrections and manual entries: Save applies them, Cancel or Back discards time corrections. Running records and unfinished time drafts survive restart in encrypted storage.</p><h3>Pauses and alarms</h3><p>Fixed clock windows count only their overlap with the recording. Separate fixed 30 minutes and manual 15 minutes total 45. A manual pause already running when a fixed window starts replaces that entire occurrence, including its unused remainder; acknowledge the notice once. Its fixed duration becomes the alarm target from the original precise manual start. Optional manual targets are 5/15/30/45 minutes, with a signal one minute before the target. Late activation never resets the start. The end-of-work alarm uses a clock time or hours since recording began, including or excluding pauses. Automatic stop is a separate option, off by default. Fixed-window and end-of-work settings apply to new recordings.</p><h3>Optional Wi-Fi start</h3><p>Select a visible SSID and save the option. No connection or login is needed. Android requires precise location permission, location services and Wi-Fi scanning; scans can be delayed. A monitoring notification stays visible; the briefcase marks an open recording. Open the app after a device restart. Every stop blocks Wi-Fi starts for the configured interval, initially 60 minutes. Afterwards a fresh sighting can start again even if the network stayed visible. Manual starts remain possible during the block, and Wi-Fi loss never finishes a record.</p><h3>History and printing</h3><p>Removing a record or month affects Notes only. It remains in encrypted trash for 30 days and can be restored; expiry or permanent removal deletes its local contents. Turning tracking off keeps history. In time-tracking settings choose the month, all or selected days and a name, then print. Android starts with A4 portrait and margins; additional rows continue on further pages. Clocks follow regional 12/24-hour settings without seconds; durations such as 168:30 do not wrap at 24 hours.</p><h3>Control the Notes timer through MCP</h3><p>First read the live timer with organizer_notes_timer_read. Use its source revision, timer revision and restore epoch for organizer_notes_timer_start, _pause, _resume or _finish. Give each command a new operation UUID; reuse that UUID only for an exact retry. Pause, resume and finish also need the active entry ID. Both devices must support the timer protocol, confirm ownership and enable time synchronization; phone-import mode permits reading only. Saved means the encrypted Notes book was actually written. Cancelling cannot recall a dispatched command. After an uncertain result, read again. Drafts remain unchanged; older peers report unsupported.</p><h3>Organizer and synchronization</h3><p>Enable time tracking in Organizer’s calendar settings. The briefcase opens recorded times without creating ordinary appointments. In the paired own-device settings, enable time tracking on both devices; use Synchronize now or automatic Wi-Fi synchronization. Note-import direction is independent. Conflicting versions remain available for review. Notes removals stay local and are not silently imported again; synchronized deletions originate in Organizer. Restoring a backup or turning tracking off disables its synchronization consent. Notes inherits Organizer’s country, region and holiday switch: without a region, all available holidays of that country apply; with holidays off, only Sundays are marked. In the planner print preview choose Time tracking, month, name and all or individual days. Print or export an editable ODS with formulas; extra rows continue with repeated headings and final totals/signature.</p>");
 gettext("<h3>New and already present</h3><p>After a file or folder import, Magnolie Notes reports how many notes were taken over and how many were already present. A duplicate is recognized from the normalized <b>title and text</b>. Filename, source, notebook and date do not distinguish it, so the same wording from another source is skipped as already present. Notes already stored without an import identity are not compared retroactively.</p><h3>Where the notes go</h3><p>Keep uses its first label or the notebook <b>Google Keep</b>. Evernote, Simplenote and Standard Notes use a notebook carrying their product name. Plain files use a recognized source or <b>Imported</b>. Existing notebook names are reused; directory names do not create a notebook hierarchy.</p><h3>ZIP safety limits</h3><p>A ZIP may contain at most <b>5,000 entries</b>, with at most <b>8 MiB</b> uncompressed per entry and <b>64 MiB</b> uncompressed altogether. Nested and multipart ZIP files are not read. A damaged archive or a violated ZIP limit rejects the complete archive instead of importing an uncertain partial result.</p><h3>When nothing arrives</h3><p><i>No readable notes were found in this file.</i> means that no nonblank title or text was produced. <i>The import failed.</i> also covers malformed or oversized ZIP files. A repeated direct share may use the first wording instead of explicitly saying that the note was a duplicate.</p><div class='achtung'><b>Keep the export until you have checked it</b>Open several imported notes, inspect notebooks, check-list marks and dates, and compare the reported number. Only then remove the original export.</div>");
 gettext("<h3>Nextcloud</h3><p>A server platform that Magnolie can use for CalDAV and CardDAV synchronization and for a separate WebDAV mailbox.</p><h3>Notification</h3><p>A message shown by the operating system, for example for a due reminder, an incoming call, or permitted Android app information.</p><h3>Pairing</h3><p>The deliberate association of two devices or branches, including identity checks and the permissions granted to that partner.</p><h3>PDF</h3><p>A fixed-layout document format used by Magnolie for print output and supported note attachments.</p><h3>Personal Sync</h3><p>Synchronization of notes, notebooks, and own tasks between one person's desktop and one phone marked as an own device; it excludes calendars, contacts, and shared Magnolienbaum entries.</p><h3>Port</h3><p>A numbered TCP or UDP endpoint through which a network service receives traffic, such as TCP 8741 for the Magnolie phone connection.</p><h3>Recovery snapshot</h3><p>An integrity-checked short-term copy of the complete main-data set inside Magnolie's data location; it is not a backup against disk or device loss.</p><h3>Recycle bin</h3><p>Local retention for supported deleted Organizer items, from which they can be restored until they expire or are removed permanently.</p><h3>RFCOMM</h3><p>A Bluetooth serial-data transport used as a permitted manual fallback for the Magnolie phone protocol; it uses no IP firewall port.</p><h3>SHA-256</h3><p>A cryptographic hash function used for file checksums, attachment identities, and other integrity checks.</p><h3>SMS</h3><p>A mobile-network text message. In Magnolie Organizer, reading and sending SMS uses KDE Connect rather than Magnolie Notes or Personal Sync.</p><h3>Synchronization</h3><p>The controlled comparison and exchange of changes between stores, with Magnolie-specific conflict, permission, and deletion safeguards.</p><h3>Time zone</h3><p>A regional rule set such as <b>Europe/Berlin</b> that relates local dates and times to absolute instants.</p><h3>TLS</h3><p>Transport Layer Security, which encrypts a network connection and authenticates its peer by certificate; KDE Connect uses mutually authenticated TLS.</p><h3>Tray</h3><p>The desktop notification area beside the clock, where Magnolie can remain available while its main window is hidden.</p><h3>UFW</h3><p>Uncomplicated Firewall, a Linux firewall frontend for which the Debian package supplies an optional Magnolie application profile.</p><h3>URI</h3><p>A standardized resource identifier, including addresses such as <b>https:</b> links and <b>sms:</b> recipients.</p><h3>vCard</h3><p>A standard contact exchange format, usually stored in a <b>.vcf</b> file and supported by Magnolie import and export.</p><h3>VPN</h3><p>A virtual private network that can securely connect devices or networks over another network; Magnolie does not create one automatically.</p><h3>Wayland</h3><p>A modern Linux display protocol under which the compositor controls details such as exact window placement.</p><h3>WebDAV</h3><p>HTTP-based access to remote files and collections; Magnolie uses a Nextcloud WebDAV folder as an optional message mailbox.</p><h3>Wi-Fi</h3><p>A wireless IP network and the preferred transport for Magnolie phone connections and Personal Sync.</p><h3>X11</h3><p>The traditional Linux window-system protocol, under which Magnolie can restore an exact window position where the window manager permits it.</p><h3>XDG</h3><p>Freedesktop.org conventions and environment variables that define standard Linux locations for data, configuration, and state files.</p><h3>ZIP</h3><p>A compressed archive format accepted by selected Magnolie imports under strict entry, size, path, and compression-ratio limits.</p>");
+gettext("<h3>One page number, several screen sections</h3><p>A numbered handbook page may fill several screen sections, depending on your screen resolution and window size. Seeing the same page number again as you turn forward is intentional: no pages are missing or duplicated. The number identifies the fixed handbook page, so the contents and topic links always refer to the same page, even after resizing the window.</p>");
 gettext("<h3>People responsible</h3><p>Use <span class='knopfwort'>People</span> to add, rename, or remove people. On the task form, you can select one or more people. Colored initials beside the task then show who is responsible.</p><h3>Filter the list</h3><p>Above the task list, you can select a person and a date range if required. <span class='knopfwort'>Clear selection</span> displays everything again. The printed output uses exactly the same people and date filters.</p><h3>Set a reminder for the due date</h3><p>When creating or editing a task, select <b>remind me on the due date</b>. The reminder appears in the morning and also names the people responsible. Reminders must be enabled globally for this to work.</p><p>Under <b>Custom notification</b>, you can also choose a lead time of one to seven days. The field starts blank and displays the gray hint <i>e.g. 1 day before</i>. You can use the due-date reminder and the custom lead time either together or separately.</p><div class='merke'><b>A task for an appointment</b>On the appointment sheet, you can link an existing task or create a new one. You can also give the new task its own reminder there.</div><div class='achtung'><b>When exporting</b>A task export includes the title, due date, priority, status, and note. People, reminders, and appointment links are retained only in a complete backup.</div>");
 gettext("<h3>Period forecast (estimate)</h3><p>Calendar display and reminders can be enabled separately. Existing entries are preserved.</p><p>Notifications are off unless enabled below.</p><ul><li>☐ Show period forecast in calendar</li><li>☐ Notify before the estimated period</li><li>☐ Report unusual cycle intervals</li></ul><p><b>Difference (days): 7</b></p><p><b>💧 ≈</b> Pale drops mark possible start days, not a predicted bleeding duration. Active predictions disappear after the estimated window if unconfirmed. Only days you confirm as actual bleeding enter future calculations. Click a drop for today or an earlier day and explicitly select the bleeding intensity actually observed. Confirmation records only that day and recalculates the estimate. Future days cannot be confirmed; Cancel leaves your records unchanged.</p><p>When hints are enabled, a separate pale dashed drop may appear at the earlier estimated start after the forecast expires. Its tooltip only says that no new start is recorded; it does not prove a missed period. Dashed drops can also explain changes or uncertainty in recorded intervals and bleeding. They never count as entries.</p><h3>Details and limitations</h3><p>Calculated from at least three starts. Bleeding entries up to three days apart form one episode. After an interval outside 14–60 days, two new intervals are needed. The window spans the shortest and longest of up to twelve recent intervals, plus two days on either side.</p><p>Estimate from your entries, not a diagnosis or contraception method. Seek medical advice for new, persistent or distressing changes.</p><h3>Pause forecasts</h3><p>Click a dashed drop and choose pregnancy, breastfeeding, medication/hormonal treatment, perimenopause/menopause, or another reason. Pregnancy initially suggests nine calendar months from recording the pause; this is not a due date. Other reasons initially have no fixed end. For every reason you can change the end date or leave it open.</p><p>The pause includes its final day; without a date it remains until explicitly ended. Forecasts, missing-start hints and cycle reminders stop, while a neutral status shows the reason and end. New observed bleeding is saved, but the pause ends only if you separately confirm it as a new period. Keep pause or cancelling that question preserves both the bleeding record and the pause. Ending the pause manually resumes planning today without creating a bleeding entry.</p><p>After resumption, only entries from the resumption day, or after the scheduled end, form the new history. Three new starts are needed; old cycles do not produce an overdue warning. Confirm bleeding as a new period only if that is how it has been identified. Bleeding during pregnancy needs prompt medical advice; bleeding after menopause also needs medical assessment. Ending a planning pause does not establish that a pregnancy or menopause has ended.</p>");
 gettext("<h3>Phone contacts (KDE Connect)</h3><p>Pair the phone in KDE Connect and allow contact access there. Open <b>Settings ▸ Synchronization ▸ Phone contacts (KDE Connect)</b> and choose <b>Import preview</b>. No Google or Microsoft account and no photo retrieval are required.</p><p>Review new contacts, the existing target and differing fields. Ambiguous matches wait for your choice. Apply saves in Organizer after a recovery snapshot; Cancel changes nothing. Repeating does not create duplicate contacts. Local changes and manually selected or removed photos are retained. Missing contacts never cause deletion. Nothing is written back to the phone. System KDE uses its synchronized contact cache.</p><h3>Google on Linux: step by step</h3><ol class='schritte'><li><b>Mint/Cinnamon:</b> open the application menu, search for <b>Online Accounts</b> and open it. Select <b>Google</b> from the providers.</li><li><b>Ubuntu/GNOME:</b> open Activities, search for <b>Online Accounts</b> and open the Settings panel. Under <b>Connect an Account</b>, select <b>Google</b>.</li><li>Follow Google's sign-in window or browser page and review the requested access. Afterwards, enable the offered <b>Calendar</b> and <b>Contacts</b> services you want to use. First check that your calendars and address books appear in the corresponding system applications.</li></ol><h3>TUXEDO/KDE</h3><ol class='schritte'><li>Open <b>System Settings ▸ Online Accounts ▸ Add New Account… ▸ Google</b>. Follow the provider's sign-in flow.</li><li>The online account alone does not prove that an Akonadi calendar or address book exists. For the KDE connection, install Magnolie's matching KDE component and the system packages <b>akonadi-server</b> and <b>kdepim-runtime</b>. KAddressBook and KOrganizer provide the address-book and calendar interfaces.</li><li>If no suitable Google resource exists, open <b>KAddressBook ▸ File ▸ New ▸ Add Address Book…</b>, select <b>Google Groupware</b> and confirm with <b>OK</b>. Complete its account setup and check the calendars and address books it provides. Use an existing suitable resource rather than adding the same account again.</li></ol><h3>Select sources in Magnolie</h3><p>Open <b>Settings ▸ Synchronization</b>, select the desired calendars and address book, then choose <b>Synchronize now</b>. A system login, a calendar, an address book and a contact photo are separate things: photos are available only when the selected source actually supplies them. Google Tasks is a separate integration and is not enabled by these steps. Labels may vary with the installed desktop version.</p>");
@@ -71,6 +74,8 @@ gettext("<h3>Vital readings</h3><p>Enter the date and time, Systolic and Diastol
 gettext("<h3>Where your data is stored</h3><p>Main Organizer data are stored in:</p><div class='beispiel'><b>Path</b>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Magnolie Organizer\\daten.json</div><p>Open <i>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Magnolie Organizer</i> in Windows Explorer to see the folder.</p><div class='achtung'><b>Know the scope</b>A main-data backup is not a complete device image. Phone identity and <b>phone.db</b>, KDE pairing, window state, and Magnolienbaum identity and queues are separate. In particular, <b>baum.json</b>, <b>baum-post.json</b>, and <b>baum-eingang.json</b> are unencrypted files outside the main password transaction. Protect your Windows account and back these files up deliberately.</div><h3>Creating and restoring a backup</h3><p><span class='knopfwort'>Create backup</span> normally creates a dated JSON file under <i>Documents\\Magnolie Organizer\\Sicherungen</i>. A password-protected main backup remains protected. Use <span class='knopfwort'>Restore backup</span> to select it in the Windows file dialog; the current data are backed up before replacement.</p>");
 gettext("<h3>Who created it?</h3><p>Magnolie Organizer and this handbook were created by <b>Maik Walter</b>.</p><h3>The license</h3><p>Both are <b>free software</b> released under version 3 of the GNU General Public License, or any later version. In plain language, this means:</p><ul><li>You may use the program as often and for as long as you wish, whether privately, with your family or in your business.</li><li>You may give copies to other people, including friends and acquaintances.</li><li>You may modify it if you have the ability and wish to do so.</li><li>If you distribute a modified version, it must again be released under the same license so that everyone benefits.</li></ul><p>It is published in the hope that it will be useful, but <i>without any warranty</i>. The complete license text is available on your computer at <i>/usr/share/common-licenses/GPL-3</i>.</p><h3>A final word</h3><div class='merke'><b>☕ I would be delighted if you bought me a coffee.</b>maik3531@gmail.com</div><div class='achtung'><b>An email address remains optional</b>If you would like to receive a Contributor-Key and have it assigned, you should provide an email address. Without an address, the key cannot be delivered, but nobody is obliged to provide one. Magnolie remains fully and unrestrictedly usable in all functions without a key. Entering a Contributor-Key only removes the <b>No valid coffee allowance</b> branding; it does not unlock any function. Magnolie remains free software under the GNU General Public License; a coffee does not purchase the software, a warranty, or a regular support contract.</div><p>You will find the same message in the Organizer under <span class='knopfwort'>Settings ▸ About</span>. You can also reopen this handbook there and check for a new version of the program.</p>");
 gettext("<h3>Why Magnolie uses KDE Connect</h3><p>Direct SMS integration in Magnolie Notes was considered. Since 2019, however, Android has restricted the permissions for reading and sending complete SMS data mainly to the system's default SMS app and narrowly approved use cases. Making Magnolie Notes the default SMS app merely for desktop integration would be disproportionate and would interfere with the phone's chosen messaging app. KDE Connect therefore handles SMS under its own Android permissions, while Magnolie Organizer uses its paired interface.</p>");
+gettext("<p class='achtung'><b>All always uses the replace method.</b> Changes made after the selected snapshot are overwritten and entries added later are removed. The additive method is available only for individual areas.</p>");
+gettext("<p class='kalender-blaettertasten'><kbd>&larr;</kbd> / <kbd>&rarr;</kbd> · Use the left and right arrow keys to turn one month, one week or two days, depending on the Calendar view. If a date or time cell has focus, the keys move focus instead. In text fields, they move the cursor.</p>");
 gettext("<p><b>KDE Connect is the only SMS path.</b> Pair it in the Organizer and accept the matching request on both devices. The desktop Organizer uses this direct interface; Magnolie Notes and <b>magnolie-phone/1</b> do not read or send SMS.</p><h3>History and live messages</h3><p>If the paired KDE Connect peer supports conversation requests, the Organizer retrieves conversation history and then adds live incoming and outgoing messages. Without that peer capability, only live messages and sending are available. Stable and content-based identities deduplicate history, retransmissions, and live events.</p><h3>Reply and compose</h3><p>Open a conversation to reply, or compose from a contact and choose the number. Sending is blocked above <b>10 segments</b>; check normalized substitutions before confirming.</p><div class='merke'><b>No READ_SMS claim</b>The KDE Connect Android app supplies messages under its own permissions. Magnolie Notes does not claim <b>READ_SMS</b>. Personal Sync never transfers SMS history.</div>");
 gettext("<p><b>Magnolie Organizer</b> is available as a complete desktop application for Linux and Windows. This edition of the handbook describes the Windows version. <b>Magnolie Notes</b> adds mobile notes, tasks, and secure exchange through the Magnolienbaum on Android phones and tablets.</p><p>Magnolie Organizer for Windows runs on <b>64-bit Windows 10 or Windows 11</b>.</p><h3>Requirements</h3><ul><li><b>Operating system:</b> Windows 10 version 2004 or later, or Windows 11, in each case 64 bit</li><li><b>Memory:</b> 2 gigabytes is enough; 4 gigabytes provides plenty of room</li><li><b>Disk space:</b> about 230 megabytes for the self-contained application, plus space for your data</li><li><b>Display:</b> at least 1024 x 768 pixels</li><li><b>WebView2:</b> Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime, present on current Windows installations</li><li><b>Internet:</b> not required for day-to-day use; only online functions such as holidays, weather, maps, and update checks need a connection</li></ul><h3>Included components</h3><p>The application includes the required .NET runtime. Python and additional interface libraries do not need to be installed.</p><div class='merke'><b>Good to know</b>The Organizer works on your computer. Your entries leave it only when you explicitly use an online or sharing function.</div>");
 gettext("<p><b>Magnolie Organizer</b> is the same program on Linux and on Windows: the same book, the same seven sections, the same twenty languages, the same data format. <b>Magnolie Notes</b> adds notes and tasks on an Android phone or tablet and exchanges them with the computer through the Magnolienbaum.</p><h3>Requirements</h3><ul><li><b>Operating system:</b> Linux Mint 21 or later, Ubuntu 22.04 or later, Debian 12 or later, Fedora, or any other distribution by way of the AppImage — or Windows 10 version 2004 or later, or Windows 11, in each case 64 bit. Magnolie Notes needs Android 8.0 or later.</li><li><b>Memory:</b> 2 gigabytes is enough; 4 gigabytes provides plenty of room.</li><li><b>Disk space:</b> about 3 megabytes on Linux, about 230 megabytes on Windows, where the application brings its own runtime. Add room for your own data: text needs very little, while embedded images and PDF files add up.</li><li><b>Display:</b> at least 1024 × 768 pixels.</li><li><b>Additional components:</b> nothing to do by hand. A Linux package installation adds the GTK and WebKit interface libraries, spell checking and encryption by itself; the AppImage and the Windows application already carry everything with them. Windows additionally uses the Microsoft Edge WebView2 runtime, which is present on current installations — and if it is missing, the program says so plainly.</li><li><b>Internet:</b> not required for day-to-day use. You need a connection to retrieve public holidays and school holidays, synchronize with online accounts, check for updates, and view maps or routes. The Magnolienbaum itself works inside your own network without any connection to the outside world.</li></ul><p>Magnolie Notes is installed from an APK file rather than from an app store. Page <b data-page='android-apk-transfer' class='knopfwort'></b> describes how to get it onto the phone safely.</p><div class='merke'><b>Good to know</b>The Organizer runs entirely on your own device. Your appointments and addresses do not leave it unless you explicitly choose to send or synchronize them.</div>");
@@ -138,6 +143,7 @@ gettext("<p>Limits make malformed or unexpectedly large input fail in a controll
 gettext("<p>Magnolie 2.0.30 can synchronize calendars and contacts with a standards-compatible DAV server. <b>CalDAV</b> carries calendars and tasks; <b>CardDAV</b> carries address books. Nextcloud is one supported server, not the name of the protocols.</p><div class='merke'><b>Can I use Baïkal?</b><b>Yes.</b> In Magnolie 2.0.30, Baïkal can be used for CalDAV and CardDAV when its HTTPS address, credentials, and DAV discovery are configured correctly.</div><ol class='schritte'><li>Create or choose a separate calendar and address book on the server. Keep a backup before the first comparison.</li><li>Open <span class='knopfwort'>Settings ▸ Synchronization</span> and enter the server's HTTPS address, user name, and a dedicated app password or DAV password.</li><li>Save the account and use <span class='knopfwort'>Test connection</span>.</li><li>Select one or more discovered calendars and, if wanted, one discovered address book.</li><li>Run <span class='knopfwort'>Synchronize now</span> and compare several entries on both sides before enabling startup synchronization.</li></ol><div class='achtung'><b>Baïkal is not a Nextcloud mailbox</b>CalDAV and CardDAV synchronization do not imply support for Magnolie's separate Magnolienbaum WebDAV mailbox. Server administration, account creation, TLS certificates, backups, and other clients remain the administrator's responsibility.</div>");
 gettext("<p>Magnolie Notes opens with its name at the top and five tabs along the bottom. This chapter concerns the two everyday tabs <span class='knopfwort'>Notes</span> and <span class='knopfwort'>Tasks</span>. The other three tabs belong to transferring and recovering data and are explained separately later.</p><h3>Portrait or landscape</h3><p>Android may display the app upright or sideways; Magnolie Notes does not lock the screen orientation. The tabs remain in one row at the bottom, and landscape does not turn the app into a two-pane desktop view. On a short screen, with a larger system font, while the keyboard is open, or when several attachments are present, the note editor scrolls vertically.</p><h3>Moving between the two everyday tabs</h3><p><b>Prepared for Magnolie Notes 1.0.14:</b> in the note and task editors, Save, the visible Back arrow and Android’s system Back button or gesture all save the current entry before closing. Do not use Back to discard changes. This describes the prepared 1.0.14 version, not the currently linked release 1.0.13. The download link will remain on 1.0.13 until a new APK is approved.</p><div class='achtung'><b>Save before rotating the device</b>The open editor and unsaved text are not promised to survive an orientation change. Save the entry before turning the phone or tablet.</div>");
 gettext("<p>Magnolie Organizer and Magnolie Notes are developed openly. The public project overview lists Maik Walter's repositories, source code, and available project information:</p><p><a href='https://gitlab.com/users/maik3531/projects'>https://gitlab.com/users/maik3531/projects</a></p><div class='merke'><b>Check the address</b>The visible address and the link destination are identical. Your web browser opens the page outside the Organizer. Releases and checksums should still be obtained only from the official location named for the respective project.</div>");
+gettext("<p>Magnolie Organizer is also listed in the <a href='https://appimage.github.io/Magnolie-Organizer'>AppImage catalogue</a>. This is a catalogue entry; check the linked release and its checksum before running an AppImage.</p>");
 gettext("<p>Magnolie Organizer stores everything you might once have written in a ring binder. It has seven sections, which you open using the colored tabs along the right-hand edge:</p><h3>The seven sections</h3><ul><li><b>Calendar</b> – Your appointments in a month, week or day view. You can enter any number of appointments for each day, with a time, note and other details.</li><li><b>Tasks</b> – Everything you need to do, with a due date, priority, people responsible and a reminder. Completed items are checked off and move to the bottom.</li><li><b>Contacts</b> – Your address book, containing postal addresses, telephone numbers and email addresses, arranged using an alphabetical index.</li><li><b>Notes</b> – Groups, notebooks and pages for anything that does not belong elsewhere, including links, images and PDF files.</li><li><b>Anniversaries</b> – Birthdays, wedding anniversaries and other fixed dates that recur every year.</li><li><b>Planner</b> – An overview of the entire year on a single sheet.</li><li><b>Health</b> – Blood pressure, blood glucose, weight and a medication plan, kept in the same encrypted data as everything else.</li></ul><p>Calendar is always available. Each of the other six can be shown or hidden under <i>Settings ▸ General ▸ Tabs</i> without losing what you have already entered.</p><div class='beispiel'><b>An everyday example</b>Mr Muller enters his dentist appointment in the <i>Calendar</i>, his granddaughter's birthday under <i>Anniversaries</i>, the garage's telephone number under <i>Contacts</i>, and “Have the tires changed” under <i>Tasks</i>.</div><p>Note pages are saved while you write. For appointments, tasks and addresses, save the completed sheet using the appropriate button, such as <i>Add appointment</i>, <i>Add</i> or <i>Save</i>. Page <b data-page='backing-up-and-restoring' class='knopfwort'></b> explains where the data is stored.</p>");
 gettext("<p>Magnolie Organizer, Magnolie Notes und dieses Handbuch sind ein freies, unabhängiges Hobbyprojekt. Sie können sie verwenden, ohne etwas kaufen zu müssen. Wenn Magnolie Ihnen hilft und Sie die Zeit unterstützen möchten, die in Entwicklung, Tests, Übersetzungen und Dokumentation fließt, würde sich Maik Walter sehr darüber freuen, wenn Sie ihm einen Kaffee spendieren.</p><figure><img class='kaffee-qr' src='kaffee-qr.png' alt='QR-Code für die freiwillige Unterstützung mit einem Kaffee'><figcaption>Scannen Sie den QR-Code, um die Seite für die freiwillige Kaffee-Unterstützung zu öffnen.</figcaption></figure><div class='merke'><b>QR-Code oder E-Mail als Alternative</b>Der QR-Code ist der direkte Weg. Falls Sie lieber PayPal verwenden oder auf anderem Weg eine freiwillige Zuwendung beziehungsweise Aufmerksamkeit zukommen lassen möchten, können Sie <i>maik3531@gmail.com</i> als Alternative zum QR-Code verwenden. Die Adresse ist keine Aufforderung für allgemeine Nachrichten.</div><div class='achtung'><b>Eine E-Mail-Adresse bleibt freiwillig</b>Wer einen Contributor-Key erhalten und zugeordnet haben möchte, sollte eine E-Mail-Adresse angeben. Ohne Adresse kann der Key nicht zugestellt werden, aber niemand ist verpflichtet, eine anzugeben. Magnolie bleibt ohne Key in sämtlichen Funktionen vollständig und uneingeschränkt nutzbar. Die Eingabe eines Contributor-Keys entfernt lediglich das Branding <b>No valid coffee allowance</b>; sie schaltet keine Funktion frei. Magnolie bleibt freie Software unter der GNU General Public License; ein Kaffee erwirbt weder die Software noch eine Gewährleistung oder einen regulären Supportvertrag.</div><p>Vielen Dank an alle, die ein Problem sorgfältig melden, eine Verbesserung vorschlagen, eine neue Version testen, eine Übersetzung verbessern oder Magnolie jemandem empfehlen, dem es nützen kann.</p>");
 gettext("<p>Magnolie gives every task a stable identity, remembers its parent, and stores its order among siblings. Personal Sync and complete Magnolie archives preserve these relationships.</p><h3>Safety rules</h3><ul><li>A task cannot become its own parent.</li><li>A task cannot be moved below one of its own descendants, because that would create a loop.</li><li>If damaged or old data names a missing parent, the task is moved to the top level rather than hidden.</li><li>Duplicate identities, negative positions, self-links, and cycles are normalized deterministically so every task remains reachable.</li><li>Deleting a parent lifts its direct children to the deleted parent's level; it does not silently delete the complete subtree.</li></ul><div class='achtung'><b>Standard task files are flatter</b>A normal ICS task export cannot preserve every Magnolie hierarchy detail. Use a complete <b>.magnolie</b> archive when parent-child structure and ordering must survive a move. Delegated tasks remain separate from your own Personal-Sync task tree.</div><p>For synchronization scope and deletion proposals, continue on page <b data-page='personal-sync' class='knopfwort'></b> and page <b data-page='personal-deletions-trash' class='knopfwort'></b>.</p>");
@@ -147,11 +153,12 @@ gettext("<p>Not every appointment has a specific time. For a day of leave, a com
 gettext("<p>Not every appointment has a specific time. For a day of leave, a company holiday, or a day when a tradesperson will arrive “at some point,” select <span class='knopfwort'>All day</span> beside the two time fields.</p><ol class='schritte'><li>Create the appointment as usual and enter its title.</li><li>Select <b>All day</b>.</li><li>If the appointment spans several days, enter the final date under <b>Through</b>.</li><li>The two time fields become pale and hatched because they are no longer needed.</li><li>Click <span class='knopfwort'>Add appointment</span>.</li></ol><p>In the appointment overview, <i>All day</i> now appears in italics instead of a time. All-day appointments are always listed <b>first</b>, above every appointment with a specific time.</p><h3>When exporting</h3><p>When you export your appointments to another program (page <b data-page='exporting-data-to-other-programs' class='knopfwort'></b>), all-day appointments are recognized there as well. Thunderbird, Outlook and the Linux Mint calendar display them as bars spanning the entire day.</p><div class='merke'><b>Changing this later</b>When you open an all-day appointment, the option is selected. Clear it and the Organizer fills the time fields with suggested times again.</div>");
 gettext("<p>On Linux, a normal <i>magnolie-organizer</i> command opens the graphical application. Only one graphical instance runs per user session: another normal start brings its window forward. <i>magnolie-organizer --tray-start</i> is the autostart form; if an instance already exists, this invocation deliberately does not bring it forward.</p><h3>Help and language</h3><p><i>--hilfe</i> and <i>--help</i> are aliases. <i>--sprache CODE</i> and <i>--language CODE</i> are aliases as well and affect only that invocation, not the saved setting. CODE may be <b>ar, be, cs, da, de, en, es, fr, hi, hsb, it, ja, nb, nl, pl, pt, ru, tr, uk, zh_CN</b>, or <b>system</b>. The selection is applied before help, diagnostics, background modes, and a newly opened interface.</p><h3>Gettext template</h3><p><i>--pot [FILE]</i> and <i>--pot-template [FILE]</i> generate a reproducible gettext template and then exit. Without FILE, the result is <i>magnolie-organizer.pot</i> in the current directory. This maintenance function takes precedence over reminder, help, and graphical modes.</p><p>The installed manual page contains the same reference: enter <i>man magnolie-organizer</i>. A missing option value or unsupported language is a command-line error and exits with status 2; template-generation failure exits with status 1.</p>");
 gettext("<p>On Windows, Magnolie can synchronize contacts with two sources.</p><h3>Local Windows Contacts folder</h3><p>Your profile's <i>Contacts</i> folder is available without signing in. Magnolie reads and writes only .contact files and does not delete unrelated files.</p><h3>Outlook.com and Microsoft 365</h3><p>Modern Microsoft contacts can optionally be connected through Microsoft Graph. Enter the client ID of your own Microsoft Entra app registration, enable device-code flow, and grant delegated <i>Contacts.ReadWrite</i>. No client secret is needed or stored.</p><p>Magnolie displays a device code and opens Microsoft's verification page. The refresh token is protected for your Windows account with Windows DPAPI and is deleted when you sign out.</p><div class='merke'><b>Without an app registration</b>Local Windows Contacts synchronization and all file imports remain available.</div>");
+gettext("<p>On the phone-apps page, <b>Magnolie Notes · F-Droid</b> opens <a href='https://f-droid.org/en/packages/io.gitlab.maik3531.magnolienotes'>the F-Droid listing</a> as an alternative to the direct APK download. Opening the link does not install the app or grant phone permissions.</p>");
 gettext("<p>Open <span class='knopfwort'>Notes</span>. The list is ordered by the most recently changed note and grouped by day, with headings such as <b>Today</b> and <b>Yesterday</b>. A row can show its symbol, title, a text preview, the time of the last change and the notebook name.</p><h3>Find a note</h3><p>Type in <span class='knopfwort'>Search</span>. Magnolie Notes looks through titles and note text without distinguishing upper- and lower-case letters. Clear the field to show the complete list again.</p><h3>Create a local note</h3><ol class='schritte'><li>Tap <span class='knopfwort'>New note</span>.</li><li>Enter a <span class='knopfwort'>Title</span> and write in <span class='knopfwort'>Write here …</span>.</li><li>Select a symbol if the ordinary Note symbol does not fit.</li><li>Use <span class='knopfwort'>Save</span> or the visible Back arrow.</li></ol><p>A new local note belongs to <b>Loose Notes</b>. Notebook names received with notes can appear in the list, but this local Android screen has no control for creating, renaming, selecting, moving or deleting notebooks.</p><div class='merke'><b>The desktop has more notebook tools</b>Groups, notebook management, links and embedded files in the Organizer are separate desktop functions. Do not look for those controls in the local Android note editor.</div>");
 gettext("<p>Open <span class='knopfwort'>Settings ▸ General ▸ Tabs</span> and switch on <span class='knopfwort'>Health</span>. The tab appears after the planners. Nothing is deleted when you hide it, but the hidden section may disappear from search and print choices until you switch it on again.</p><h3>Views and units</h3><p>Health has four subviews: <span class='knopfwort'>Vital readings</span>, <span class='knopfwort'>Medication plan</span>, the optional blood-sugar table, and <span class='knopfwort'>History</span>. Under <span class='knopfwort'>Settings ▸ Calendar ▸ Health</span>, you can show or hide each view. If imported settings hide all four, Vital readings is restored so the section can still open.</p><p>The same settings choose kg or lb, cm or in, °C or °F, and mmol/L or mg/dL. <span class='knopfwort'>According to language</span> follows the region of the selected language. Changing a selector converts the entered and saved values for display; it does not reinterpret them. Finish or discard an edited row before changing a unit.</p><h3>Calendar planning</h3><p>The switches for vital readings, medication and blood sugar add general planning marks to the calendar. Choose a first date and an interval from 1 to 365 days. The Today marker and other calendar entries remain separate. Clicking a vital-reading or blood-sugar mark opens a row with its date; a medication mark opens the plan. These marks are not reminders and have no completion state.</p><div class='achtung'><b>Protect sensitive data</b>Health records are stored locally with the rest of the Organizer data. They are encrypted at rest only when password protection is enabled. Printed pages and ODS exports are separate files; protect or remove them yourself.</div>");
